---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import ArticleSchema from "../../components/schema/ArticleSchema.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";

const tableOfContents = [
  { id: "intro", label: "Introduction" },
  { id: "soc2-third-party", label: "What SOC 2 Says About Third-Party Tools" },
  {
    id: "compliance-checklist",
    label: "AI Coding Assistant Compliance Checklist",
  },
  { id: "tool-breakdown", label: "Tool-by-Tool Breakdown" },
  { id: "open-source-security", label: "The Open-Source Security Question" },
  {
    id: "acceptable-use-policy",
    label: "Building an AI Acceptable Use Policy",
  },
  { id: "conclusion", label: "Conclusion" },
];
---

<Layout
  title="Using AI Coding Tools While Staying SOC 2 Compliant"
  description="Can you use Claude, Copilot, or Cursor and stay SOC 2 compliant? Here's the practical checklist for startups using AI coding assistants in 2026."
>
  <ArticleSchema
    title="Using AI Coding Tools While Staying SOC 2 Compliant"
    description="Can you use Claude, Copilot, or Cursor and stay SOC 2 compliant? Here's the practical checklist for startups using AI coding assistants in 2026."
    url="https://www.probo.com/hub/ai-coding-tools-soc2-compliance"
    image="https://www.probo.com/hub/ai-coding-tools-soc2-compliance.jpg"
    datePublished="2026-01-28"
    dateModified="2026-08-31"
  />
  <main class="container my-10 sm:my-20">
    <Breadcrumbs
      items={[
        { label: "Hub", href: "/hub" },
        { label: "Using AI Coding Tools While Staying SOC 2 Compliant" },
      ]}
      class="mb-6"
    />
    <!-- Header -->
    <header class="mx-auto mb-12 max-w-[900px] text-center sm:mb-16">
      <h1
        class="text-foreground mb-6 text-[2.5rem] leading-[1.1] font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em] sm:text-[3.5rem]"
      >
        Using
        <span class="highlight-text">AI Coding Tools</span><br /> While Staying SOC
        2 Compliant
      </h1>
      <p class="text-muted-foreground mb-8 text-lg">
        Your engineering team just discovered Claude Code can refactor legacy
        systems in minutes. Your developers are begging to use GitHub Copilot.
        And that new hire won't stop talking about how Cursor transformed their
        productivity at their last job. But you're SOC 2 compliant—or working
        toward it. Can you actually use these AI coding assistants without
        blowing up your compliance posture?
      </p>
      <img
        src="/hub/ai-coding-tools-soc2-compliance.jpg"
        alt="Using AI Coding Tools While Staying SOC 2 Compliant"
        class="mx-auto w-full max-w-[800px] rounded-2xl shadow-lg"
      />
    </header>

    <!-- Content with TOC -->
    <div class="flex flex-col gap-10 lg:flex-row lg:gap-16">
      <!-- Table of Contents - Sidebar -->
      <aside class="shrink-0 lg:w-56">
        <nav class="lg:sticky lg:top-24">
          <div class="flex flex-col gap-2">
            {
              tableOfContents.map((item) => (
                <button
                  type="button"
                  data-section={item.id}
                  class="toc-link cursor-pointer border-none bg-transparent p-0 text-left text-sm leading-5 text-[#6b716a] transition-colors hover:text-[#141e12]"
                >
                  {item.label}
                </button>
              ))
            }
          </div>
        </nav>
      </aside>

      <!-- Main Content -->
      <article class="max-w-[720px] flex-1">
        <!-- Introduction -->
        <section id="intro" class="mb-12 scroll-mt-24">
          <h2
            class="text-foreground mb-6 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.01em] sm:text-[1.75rem]"
          >
            The AI Tool Dilemma Every Compliant Startup Faces
          </h2>
          <div class="text-muted-foreground space-y-4 leading-relaxed">
            <p>
              That compliance badge represents months of work, thousands of
              dollars, and the trust of every enterprise customer in your
              pipeline. So here's the question keeping CTOs up at night: Can you
              actually use these AI coding assistants without blowing up your
              compliance posture?
            </p>
            <p>
              The short answer is yes. But the practical answer requires
              understanding exactly what SOC 2 compliance requirements demand,
              how different AI tools handle your code, and what controls you
              need to implement.
            </p>
            <p>
              This isn't theoretical hand-wringing. With the compliance
              automation industry exploding in 2026, the intersection of AI
              tools and compliance frameworks is where the real work happens.
              Let's break down exactly what you need to do.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <hr class="border-border-low mb-12" />

        <!-- What SOC 2 Actually Says About Third-Party Tools -->
        <section id="soc2-third-party" class="mb-12 scroll-mt-24">
          <h2
            class="text-foreground mb-6 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.01em] sm:text-[1.75rem]"
          >
            What SOC 2 Actually Says About Third-Party Tools
          </h2>
          <div class="text-muted-foreground space-y-4 leading-relaxed">
            <p>
              Before diving into specific AI tools, let's clarify what SOC 2
              requirements actually demand when it comes to third-party
              software. Spoiler: SOC 2 doesn't ban AI coding assistants. It
              requires you to manage them properly.
            </p>

            <!-- Vendor Management Requirement -->
            <div class="mt-8">
              <h3 class="text-foreground mb-3 text-lg font-semibold">
                The Vendor Management Requirement
              </h3>
              <p class="mb-4">
                SOC 2's Trust Services Criteria include specific requirements
                around vendor and third-party risk management. Under the Common
                Criteria (CC9.2), organizations must assess and manage risks
                associated with vendors and business partners.
              </p>
              <p class="mb-3">
                Here's what that means in practice for your SOC 2 checklist:
              </p>
              <ul class="ml-0 list-none space-y-3">
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span
                    ><strong class="text-foreground"
                      >Due diligence documentation:</strong
                    >
                    You need evidence that you evaluated the AI tool's security posture
                    before adoption</span
                  >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span
                    ><strong class="text-foreground"
                      >Contractual protections:</strong
                    >
                    Your agreements should address data handling, security commitments,
                    and breach notification</span
                  >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span
                    ><strong class="text-foreground">Ongoing monitoring:</strong
                    >
                    Annual (at minimum) reviews of vendor compliance status</span
                  >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span
                    ><strong class="text-foreground">Risk assessment:</strong>
                    Documented analysis of what data the tool accesses and the associated
                    risks</span
                  >
                </li>
              </ul>
              <p class="mt-4">
                The good news? Major AI coding assistants like Claude, Copilot,
                and Cursor are built by companies that understand enterprise
                requirements. They've invested heavily in security
                certifications and enterprise-grade controls. Your job is
                documenting that you verified this—not proving they're secure
                from scratch.
              </p>
              <p class="mt-4">
                For a deeper dive into managing vendor relationships during your
                compliance journey, check out our
                <a
                  href="/hub/third-party-risk-management"
                  class="text-[#5d770d] hover:underline"
                  >vendor management resources</a
                >.
              </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Data Processing and Confidentiality Controls -->
            <div class="mt-8">
              <h3 class="text-foreground mb-3 text-lg font-semibold">
                Data Processing and Confidentiality Controls
              </h3>
              <p class="mb-4">
                The confidentiality principle in SOC 2 requirements gets
                specific about protecting sensitive information. When your
                developers use AI coding assistants, code snippets—potentially
                containing proprietary logic, API keys, or customer data
                patterns—flow to external systems.
              </p>
              <p class="mb-3">Your controls must address:</p>
              <ol class="list-inside list-decimal space-y-2">
                <li>
                  <strong class="text-foreground"
                    >What data leaves your environment:</strong
                  >
                  Which repositories, files, or code sections can developers use with
                  AI tools?
                </li>
                <li>
                  <strong class="text-foreground"
                    >How that data is processed:</strong
                  >
                  Does the AI provider retain your code? For how long? For what purposes?
                </li>
                <li>
                  <strong class="text-foreground"
                    >Who can access that data:</strong
                  >
                  What are the provider's internal access controls?
                </li>
                <li>
                  <strong class="text-foreground"
                    >Where that data resides:</strong
                  >
                  Does the provider's data residency align with your commitments to
                  customers?
                </li>
              </ol>
              <p class="mt-4">
                This is where the enterprise vs. consumer tier distinction
                becomes critical—and where many startups make
                compliance-threatening mistakes.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <hr class="border-border-low mb-12" />

        <!-- AI Coding Assistant Compliance Checklist -->
        <section id="compliance-checklist" class="mb-12 scroll-mt-24">
          <h2
            class="text-foreground mb-6 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.01em] sm:text-[1.75rem]"
          >
            AI Coding Assistant Compliance Checklist
          </h2>
          <div class="text-muted-foreground space-y-4 leading-relaxed">
            <p>
              Let's get practical. Here's your SOC 2 checklist specifically for
              AI coding tool adoption. Document each item, and you'll have
              audit-ready evidence of proper vendor management.
            </p>

            <!-- 1. Data Residency and Storage Policies -->
            <div class="mt-8">
              <h3 class="text-foreground mb-3 text-lg font-semibold">
                1. Data Residency and Storage Policies
              </h3>
              <p class="mb-4">
                Before approving any AI coding assistant, answer these questions
                in writing:
              </p>

              <div class="mb-4">
                <p class="text-foreground mb-2 font-semibold">
                  Where does your code go?
                </p>
                <ul class="ml-0 list-none space-y-2">
                  <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                    <span> Identify the provider's data center locations</span>
                  </li>
                  <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                    <span>
                      Confirm alignment with any customer contractual
                      requirements (especially for EU customers under GDPR)</span
                    >
                  </li>
                  <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                    <span>
                      Document whether you can select specific regions for data
                      processing</span
                    >
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </div>

              <div class="mb-4">
                <p class="text-foreground mb-2 font-semibold">
                  How long is it stored?
                </p>
                <ul class="ml-0 list-none space-y-2">
                  <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                    <span>
                      Distinguish between transient processing (code analyzed
                      then discarded) and persistent storage</span
                    >
                  </li>
                  <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                    <span>
                      Identify any caching mechanisms and their duration</span
                    >
                  </li>
                  <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                    <span>
                      Understand backup and disaster recovery implications</span
                    >
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </div>

              <div class="mb-4">
                <p class="text-foreground mb-2 font-semibold">
                  What's the legal jurisdiction?
                </p>
                <ul class="ml-0 list-none space-y-2">
                  <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                    <span>
                      Confirm which country's laws govern data handling</span
                    >
                  </li>
                  <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                    <span>
                      Assess implications for government access requests</span
                    >
                  </li>
                  <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                    <span>
                      Document any relevant data protection certifications (SOC
                      2, ISO 27001, etc.)</span
                    >
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </div>

              <p class="mt-4">
                For most enterprise-tier AI tools, you'll find this information
                in their Trust Centers or security documentation. If you can't
                find clear answers, that's a red flag.
              </p>
            </div>

            <!-- 2. Code Snippet Retention Settings -->
            <div class="mt-8">
              <h3 class="text-foreground mb-3 text-lg font-semibold">
                2. Code Snippet Retention Settings
              </h3>
              <p class="mb-4">
                This is where SOC 2 compliance requirements get granular.
                Different AI tools have dramatically different approaches to
                code retention:
              </p>
              <ul class="ml-0 list-none space-y-3">
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span
                    ><strong class="text-foreground"
                      >Zero-retention options:</strong
                    >
                    Some enterprise plans offer settings where your code is processed
                    but never stored for model training or improvement. This is the
                    gold standard for compliance-conscious organizations.</span
                  >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span
                    ><strong class="text-foreground"
                      >Training data policies:</strong
                    >
                    Understand whether your code could be used to improve the AI model.
                    Most enterprise tiers explicitly exclude customer code from training
                    data—but you need to verify this in writing.</span
                  >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span
                    ><strong class="text-foreground"
                      >Conversation history:</strong
                    >
                    Even if code isn't retained for training, chat histories or session
                    logs might persist. Know where these live and how to purge them
                    if needed.</span
                  >
                </li>
              </ul>
              <p
                class="border-border-low mt-4 flex items-start gap-2 rounded-lg border bg-[#f8f9f7] p-4"
              >
                <span>💡</span>
                <span
                  ><strong class="text-foreground">Action item:</strong>
                  Create a configuration checklist for each approved AI tool. When
                  developers onboard, they should configure retention settings before
                  writing their first prompt.</span
                >
              </p>
            </div>

            <!-- 3. Enterprise vs. Consumer Tier Differences -->
            <div class="mt-8">
              <h3 class="text-foreground mb-3 text-lg font-semibold">
                3. Enterprise vs. Consumer Tier Differences
              </h3>
              <p class="mb-4">
                Here's where startups often stumble: the free or consumer tier
                of an AI coding assistant typically has very different data
                handling practices than the enterprise version.
              </p>

              <div class="mb-4">
                <p class="text-foreground mb-2 font-semibold">
                  Consumer/Free tiers commonly:
                </p>
                <ul class="ml-0 list-none space-y-2">
                  <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                    <span>Retain code snippets for model training</span>
                  </li>
                  <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                    <span>Offer limited or no audit logging</span>
                  </li>
                  <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                    <span>Lack single sign-on (SSO) integration</span>
                  </li>
                  <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                    <span> Provide no data processing agreements (DPAs)</span>
                  </li>
                  <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                    <span>Have minimal access controls</span>
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </div>

              <div class="mb-4">
                <p class="text-foreground mb-2 font-semibold">
                  Enterprise tiers typically include:
                </p>
                <ul class="ml-0 list-none space-y-2">
                  <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                    <span>Explicit data retention controls</span>
                  </li>
                  <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                    <span>Comprehensive audit logs</span>
                  </li>
                  <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                    <span>SSO and SCIM provisioning</span>
                  </li>
                  <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                    <span>Business Associate Agreements or DPAs</span>
                  </li>
                  <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                    <span>Role-based access controls</span>
                  </li>
                  <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                    <span>Dedicated security reviews</span>
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </div>

              <p class="mt-4">
                The cost difference between tiers often seems steep—until you
                compare it to the cost of failing an audit or losing an
                enterprise deal. Understanding the
                <a href="/hub/soc2" class="text-[#5d770d] hover:underline"
                  >true cost of SOC 2 compliance</a
                >
                helps put these tool investments in perspective.
              </p>
              <p
                class="border-border-low mt-4 flex items-start gap-2 rounded-lg border bg-[#f8f9f7] p-4"
              >
                <span>💡</span>
                <span
                  ><strong class="text-foreground">Pro tip:</strong>
                  If budget constraints force you toward consumer tiers, implement
                  compensating controls. Restrict which repositories can be used with
                  AI tools, require
                  <a
                    href="/blog/2025-11-09-do-you-need-code-reviews"
                    class="text-[#5d770d] hover:underline">code review</a
                  >{" "}
                  before any AI-assisted commits, and document these limitations in
                  your policies.</span
                >
              </p>
            </div>

            <!-- 4. Audit Log and Access Control Requirements -->
            <div class="mt-8">
              <h3 class="text-foreground mb-3 text-lg font-semibold">
                4. Audit Log and Access Control Requirements
              </h3>
              <p class="mb-4">
                Your auditor will ask: "How do you know who used AI tools, when,
                and with what code?"
              </p>

              <div class="mb-4">
                <p class="text-foreground mb-2 font-semibold">
                  Strong audit log capabilities should include:
                </p>
                <ul class="ml-0 list-none space-y-2">
                  <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                    <span
                      ><strong class="text-foreground"
                        >User identification:</strong
                      >
                      Which team member initiated each session</span
                    >
                  </li>
                  <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                    <span
                      ><strong class="text-foreground"
                        >Timestamp records:</strong
                      >
                      When AI tools were accessed</span
                    >
                  </li>
                  <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                    <span
                      ><strong class="text-foreground">Query logging:</strong>
                      What prompts or code snippets were submitted (or at minimum,
                      that queries occurred)</span
                    >
                  </li>
                  <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                    <span
                      ><strong class="text-foreground"
                        >Response tracking:</strong
                      >
                      What the AI returned (for sensitive use cases)</span
                    >
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </div>

              <div class="mb-4">
                <p class="text-foreground mb-2 font-semibold">
                  For access controls, document:
                </p>
                <ul class="ml-0 list-none space-y-2">
                  <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                    <span
                      ><strong class="text-foreground"
                        >Approval workflows:</strong
                      >
                      Who authorizes AI tool access for new team members</span
                    >
                  </li>
                  <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                    <span
                      ><strong class="text-foreground"
                        >Role-based permissions:</strong
                      >
                      Which teams or individuals can use which tools</span
                    >
                  </li>
                  <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                    <span
                      ><strong class="text-foreground"
                        >Offboarding procedures:</strong
                      >
                      How access is revoked when employees leave</span
                    >
                  </li>
                  <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                    <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                    <span
                      ><strong class="text-foreground"
                        >Regular access reviews:</strong
                      >
                      Quarterly verification that only appropriate personnel have
                      access</span
                    >
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </div>

              <p class="mt-4">
                If your chosen AI tool lacks native audit logging, implement
                wrapper solutions or require developers to log usage manually.
                It's not elegant, but it's compliant.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <hr class="border-border-low mb-12" />

        <!-- Tool-by-Tool Breakdown -->
        <section id="tool-breakdown" class="mb-12 scroll-mt-24">
          <h2
            class="text-foreground mb-6 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.01em] sm:text-[1.75rem]"
          >
            Tool-by-Tool Breakdown: Claude, Copilot, Cursor, and More
          </h2>
          <div class="text-muted-foreground space-y-4 leading-relaxed">
            <p>
              Let's examine the major AI coding assistants through a SOC 2
              compliance lens. Note that capabilities evolve rapidly—verify
              current offerings before making decisions.
            </p>

            <!-- Claude -->
            <div class="mt-8">
              <h3 class="text-foreground mb-3 text-lg font-semibold">
                Claude (Anthropic)
              </h3>
              <ul class="ml-0 list-none space-y-2">
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span>
                    Anthropic has achieved SOC 2 Type II certification for their
                    enterprise offerings</span
                  >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span>
                    Claude for Enterprise includes zero-retention options for
                    prompts and outputs</span
                  >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span>
                    API usage can be configured with specific data handling
                    requirements</span
                  >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span>
                    Enterprise plans include SSO, audit logs, and dedicated
                    security reviews</span
                  >
                </li>
              </ul>
              <p class="mt-3">
                For SOC 2 compliance, the key is ensuring you're on an
                appropriate tier with the right configurations enabled. Consumer
                Claude usage should be explicitly prohibited in your acceptable
                use policy unless compensating controls exist.
              </p>
            </div>

            <!-- GitHub Copilot -->
            <div class="mt-8">
              <h3 class="text-foreground mb-3 text-lg font-semibold">
                GitHub Copilot
              </h3>
              <p class="mb-3">
                As the most widely adopted AI coding assistant, Copilot has
                mature enterprise features:
              </p>
              <ul class="ml-0 list-none space-y-2">
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span>
                    Copilot Enterprise and Copilot Business offer code retention
                    controls</span
                  >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span>
                    Organizations can disable code snippet collection for model
                    training</span
                  >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span>
                    Integration with GitHub's existing audit log infrastructure</span
                  >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span>
                    SOC 2 Type II certified as part of GitHub's broader
                    compliance program</span
                  >
                </li>
              </ul>
              <p class="mt-3">
                The critical setting: ensure "Suggestions matching public code"
                blocking is enabled if you're concerned about license compliance
                alongside security.
              </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Cursor -->
            <div class="mt-8">
              <h3 class="text-foreground mb-3 text-lg font-semibold">Cursor</h3>
              <p class="mb-3">
                The newer entrant has rapidly added enterprise capabilities:
              </p>
              <ul class="ml-0 list-none space-y-2">
